WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.141 --> 00:00:03.015
Hi, I'm Jen Sievers.

2
00:00:03.015 --> 00:00:07.130
I'm a nurse on the Med-Surg unit at
Kestrel Falls Community Hospital.

3
00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:10.747
Laura Hemmingsen asked me to talk about my
work and how I feel about replacing

4
00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:13.428
Plexmoor, so I'm staying a few
minutes before my shift.

5
00:00:13.428 --> 00:00:17.383
I've been a nurse on Med-Surg for
seven years, all evenings.

6
00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:18.453
Honestly, I'm of two minds.

7
00:00:18.453 --> 00:00:20.417
Some of Plexmoor drives me crazy.

8
00:00:20.417 --> 00:00:24.819
Scanning meds at the bedside is slow, and the
eMAR freezes at the worst times,

9
00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:27.073
like right at the evening med pass.

10
00:00:27.073 --> 00:00:29.212
If the new system fixes that,
I'm all for it.

11
00:00:29.212 --> 00:00:33.708
When the eMAR freezes, you stand there with the
scanner and the patient's cup of

12
00:00:33.708 --> 00:00:36.848
pills, and you wait.

13
00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:39.277
Sometimes thirty seconds, sometimes
a couple of minutes.

14
00:00:39.277 --> 00:00:40.848
Multiply that by five or six patients,

15
00:00:40.848 --> 00:00:44.847
and the eight o'clock meds turn into
the eight forty-five meds.

16
00:00:44.847 --> 00:00:46.414
Some nurses give the meds and
chart afterward,

17
00:00:46.414 --> 00:00:48.927
which defeats the whole point of scanning.

18
00:00:48.927 --> 00:00:52.657
I try not to, but I understand
why people do it.

19
00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:53.974
Here's a typical evening.

20
00:00:53.974 --> 00:00:58.844
I come on at three, get report, and I usually
have five patients, sometimes six.

21
00:00:58.844 --> 00:01:00.509
Evenings are busy.

22
00:01:00.509 --> 00:01:03.640
Admissions come up from the ED, surgeons'
patients come back from recovery,

23
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.581
and families visit after work, so
there are a lot of questions.

24
00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:12.668
On a busy evening I might spend more time looking
at the screen than at my patients.

25
00:01:12.668 --> 00:01:17.091
Assessments, I and O, care plans, the
fall risk score, education.

26
00:01:17.091 --> 00:01:19.359
A lot of it is clicking boxes
that nobody reads.

27
00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:24.494
My biggest worry about a new system is that it
makes charting worse for the first

28
00:01:24.494 --> 00:01:27.355
few months, while we're all learning it,
and the patients feel it.

29
00:01:27.355 --> 00:01:28.694
We'll be slower.

30
00:01:28.694 --> 00:01:31.367
We'll be looking for buttons.

31
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:36.408
And our staffing won't change just because
we're learning a new computer.

32
00:01:36.408 --> 00:01:39.133
The patients don't get less
sick during go-live.

33
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:42.440
What would help me most is
training that fits my shift.

34
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.797
Every training they've ever scheduled
is at nine in the morning.

35
00:01:45.797 --> 00:01:47.802
I work three to eleven-thirty.

36
00:01:47.802 --> 00:01:52.203
Either I come in on my day off, or I come in
exhausted after a short night.

37
00:01:52.203 --> 00:01:53.975
Neither one is good learning.

38
00:01:53.975 --> 00:01:57.138
Put sessions in the late
afternoon, or record them,

39
00:01:57.138 --> 00:01:59.891
or give us practice time on the
unit when it's quiet.

40
00:01:59.891 --> 00:02:01.170
And pay us for it.

41
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.663
If it's on our own time, people will
skip it and then struggle.

42
00:02:04.663 --> 00:02:08.622
What I don't want is to be told on go-live
day where the buttons moved.

43
00:02:08.622 --> 00:02:09.698
Show us early.

44
00:02:09.698 --> 00:02:11.990
Let us practice with fake patients first.

45
00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:16.413
A training environment where I can scan a fake
med and chart a fake assessment,

46
00:02:16.413 --> 00:02:18.677
and nothing breaks if I make a mistake.

47
00:02:18.677 --> 00:02:20.471
And make it look like our unit.

48
00:02:20.471 --> 00:02:22.390
Generic examples don't help.

49
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:26.195
When we go live, I want someone on my
shift who knows the system.

50
00:02:26.195 --> 00:02:27.558
Not just day shift.

51
00:02:27.558 --> 00:02:31.337
Every time IT sends people to help,
they're gone by five o'clock,

52
00:02:31.337 --> 00:02:33.490
and that's when my evening is
just starting.

53
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:37.117
A nurse who's been trained extra, who
can show me the shortcut,

54
00:02:37.117 --> 00:02:40.641
would be worth more than a phone number.

55
00:02:40.641 --> 00:02:41.550
Would I be one of those people?

56
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:42.150
Maybe.

57
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:45.539
If I got the training and the
time to do it right,

58
00:02:45.539 --> 00:02:49.188
and somebody covered some of my
patients during go-live.

59
00:02:49.188 --> 00:02:53.235
I don't want to be a super user who
also has six patients.

60
00:02:53.235 --> 00:02:57.353
And to keep the evening nurses
informed, come to our huddle.

61
00:02:57.353 --> 00:02:59.104
We have one at three-fifteen,
right after report.

62
00:02:59.104 --> 00:03:03.863
Five minutes, once a week, with real updates:
what's decided, what's changed,

63
00:03:03.863 --> 00:03:05.183
when training starts.

64
00:03:05.183 --> 00:03:05.782
Emails get lost.

65
00:03:05.183 --> 00:03:07.762
Most of us read them on our
break, if at all.

66
00:03:07.762 --> 00:03:13.673
And when we tell you something isn't working,
tell us what you did about it.

67
00:03:13.673 --> 00:03:17.305
If we speak up and nothing happens,
people stop speaking up.

68
00:03:17.305 --> 00:03:19.007
Closing that loop really matters.

69
00:03:19.007 --> 00:03:20.281
Nurses notice everything.

70
00:03:20.281 --> 00:03:20.881
Use that.

